# API

Supports `.project-overview.mdc` and `architecture.mdc`. HTTP and WebSocket are transport, not the application.

## Thin handlers

```
validate input → authenticate → authorize → tenant context → use case → map result
```

Never: Controller → Prisma, Controller → OpenAI, Controller → state transitions. Python FastAPI inbound adapters in `apps/ai` follow the same rule — they call AI use cases, they do not contain provider SDK calls.

## Validation and errors

Validate every request, webhook, and WebSocket message at the boundary.

Map domain/application errors to HTTP/WebSocket payloads. Do not leak stack traces, Prisma messages, or provider details.

## Auth

Authentication ≠ authorization. Enforce permissions server-side (`conversation.read`, `conversation.assign`, `conversation.escalate`, `ticket.manage`, `knowledge.manage`, `analytics.view`).

## Real-time

WebSockets/SSE for new messages, AI responses, assignment, escalation, ticket updates, typing indicators. Transport code only publishes what use cases/events already decided.

## Async vs sync AI

Do not block the TypeScript request on document processing, embeddings, RAG ingestion, notifications, or other long/retryable work. Accept, enqueue/event, return.

Request-scoped AI (response generation, classification, summarization, AI decisions) may call the Python FastAPI service synchronously over HTTP via `AIServicePort`. Do not implement that AI work in the TypeScript handler.
